rms of  typesetting in the editor  now i want to talk a little bit about  the the things we were just working on  over the last year or so and what our  our current focus is  in terms of development one thing that's  certainly  in our focus is graphics rendering and  improving the performance and also uh  the interactivity of that  what we're doing is uh basically  implementing  the whole graphics rendering pipeline  in javascript as well so far for most  graphics  we still rely on data that's generated  on the server and then translated into  svg graphics on the client  but increasingly we're supporting more  and more  graphics primitives and directives and  we can  render them directly in the browser  based on the graphics box expression  what that allows us to do in combination  with our client-side evaluator of  expressions  is you know things like things like this  pretty dynamic animation of the planets  in this case  that runs purely on the client side even  though it's fully  implemented in wolfram language so it's  quite a bit of code so i won't go into  the details but  it's basically just constructing  graphics on the fly  in an animator yeah that's  that's the whole project to to implement  graphics and  that comes with supporting dynamics  inside graphics  at a much better level  thing we're working on is what we call  interactive graphics  and the idea here is that for certain  plots  and perhaps other graphics we add  various interactive effects  to make them nicer to look at and nicer  to work with  so for a plot like this we might add  highlights of the nearest point and  also add drop lines so you can see on  the axis  where you're at right now and this is  sort of a combination  of things that happen in the kernel when  the  graphic is generated and corresponding  mechanisms that  take various annotations produced by the  kernel and  and turn them into interactive effects  like this  so some of this is hopefully coming in  12 2 and  then there's c